.SH "NAME
Tips and tricks

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Socket activation is a feature of systemd that allows a service to be started and run in the foreground, and then switched to background operation, or started in background operation immediately. This is done by adding the socket activation setting to the service configuration file. See \fBsystemd\.socket(5)\fR for details.

.SH "NAME
Tips and tricks

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
systemd has some graphical user interface configuration tools for managing the system, including systemd-generate-home.service, systemd-generate-vconsole.service, and others.

.SH "NAME
Running services after the network is up

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
You can use the \fBAfter=\fR option in the service file to delay the start of a service until the network is available.

.SH "NAME
Enable installed units by default

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
You can use the \fBDefaultDependencies=\fR option in the service file to enable a service to start when the system starts up.

.SH "NAME
Sandboxing

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
systemd provides sandboxing for applications. This feature is used to isolate applications from each other and the rest of the system.

.SH "NAME
Control user unit as root

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
You can use \fBsystemd-runas\*(lq\*(bR to control a user unit.

